  "answer_text": "If you are reusing the same drinking glass for a week and only refilling it with filtered water from your fridge, the whitish film that develops is a combination of two things: **mineral deposits** and **biofilm**. \n\nHere is exactly what is happening inside your glass and why your fridge filter isn't stopping it.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Hard Water Mineral Deposits (Limescale)\nEven though your water is filtered, it still contains dissolved minerals like calcium and magnesium. \n* **Why your filter doesn't stop it:** Most refrigerator water filters use activated carbon. Carbon filters are excellent at removing chlorine, bad tastes, odors, and certain heavy metals, but **they do not remove dissolved minerals**. To remove minerals, you would need a water softener or a reverse osmosis system.\n* **How the film forms:** As you drink the water or as it sits, small amounts of water naturally evaporate from the sides of the glass. When the water turns to vapor, it leaves the calcium and magnesium behind. Over a week of constant refills, these minerals concentrate and bond to the glass, forming a chalky, whitish film (calcium carbonate).\n\n### 2. Biofilm (Bacteria, Backwash, and Dust)\nEvery time you take a sip of water, a tiny amount of saliva and backwash travels from your mouth back into the glass. \n* **How the film forms:** Your mouth is home to hundreds of harmless, natural bacteria. When introduced to a wet, room-temperature environment, these bacteria begin to multiply. Additionally, tiny dust particles and airborne mold or yeast spores from the room settle in the glass. \n* Over the course of a week, these microorganisms secrete a sticky, protective substance that anchors them to the glass, creating a slimy, cloudy matrix called **biofilm**. \n\n---\n\n### How to tell which one is dominating your glass\nYou can easily test what the film is made of using a quick household trick:\n* **Dip a sponge or cloth in white vinegar and rub the film.** \n* If the film **dissolves instantly**, it is almost entirely **mineral buildup** (the acid in vinegar breaks down calcium).\n* If it is **stubborn and slimy**, you are dealing with a heavy **biofilm**.\n\n### Is it safe?\nWhile drinking your own mouth bacteria generally won't make you sick immediately, allowing those bacteria to fester and multiply at room temperature for a week is not highly sanitary. If anyone else drinks from your glass, they could easily catch a virus or bacteria you are carrying. Furthermore, airborne mold or opportunistic bacteria can colonize the glass over a week, which can cause mild stomach upset. \n\n### How to prevent it\n* **Wash with soap and scrub:** Simply rinsing the glass with tap water won't break the chemical bonds of minerals or the sticky shield of a biofilm. You need to use dish soap and physically scrub it.\n* **Get a fresh glass more often:** It is highly recommended to wash your glass (or switch to a clean one) every 1 to 2 days. \n* **If you have a stubborn mineral film:** Soak the glass in a mixture of 50/50 warm water and white vinegar for 15 minutes, then wash normally with dish soap.",
